ld mean Northern Ireland  abiding by European trade laws until a  better option came along mrs. may sign  that in December 2017 but the discontent  slowly grew by the summer the strain was  obvious so she summoned her cabinet for  a meeting  amidst the quiet and solitude of the  Prime Minister's country residence  Checker's this said mrs. May was her  view of the UK's future relationship  with Europe it was time for her team to  get behind it and not all of them did  next day David Davis resigned as brexit  secretary saying the Checker's plan was  too soft he was followed out of cabinet  by Boris Johnson the Foreign Secretary a  lightning rod for many in the leave  campaign who departed saying the UK was  heading for the status of a European  colony  dissent became endemic dominate robbed  took over as brexit secretary and then  resigned a few months later more  resignations followed in yet the prime  minister persevered now critics were now  emboldened they saw the Prime Minister  as a reluctant director tear a remainer  who never really believed in the brexit  she was negotiating you have streams of  talent within the Conservative Party who  would be very capable of support of  leading a proper braixen the withdrawal  agreement published the future  relationship set out and widely derided  and nothing could change the  parliamentary arithmetic there was no  majority for the deal everyone knew if  we went ahead and held the votes  tomorrow the deal would be rejected she  set off on a frantic dash around Europe  to drum up support to try to eke out  fresh concessions a journey curtailed by  summons back to Downing Street to face a  vote of no-confidence from her own  colleagues and now it comes to this the  eyes of the world trained on Westminster  intrigue and yes baffled by how the path  to brexit could have become so  extraordinarily difficult  Adam Parsons Sky News
 

